        "stageRecord": "The matrix predicate selects the dass-clause as its propositional complement and assigns its external argument to Der Forscher in Spec-IP. This analysis uses the classical GB option of base-generating external subjects in Spec-IP, rather than deriving them from VP-internal positions. German VP and IP are head-final: the matrix complement precedes sagte in VP, and the matrix inflectional position follows VP. The initially empty matrix C will host the finite verb in the declarative V2 derivation. Within the embedded CP, overt dass occupies C, die Studentin occupies Spec-IP, and den schwierigen Bericht is the complement of participial gelesen. The finite perfect auxiliary hat is base-generated in embedded I; this analysis does not introduce a separate auxiliary VP. Determiners head DPs, and schwierigen is an AP left-adjoined to the object's N-bar, modifying Bericht. First, sagte assigns the researcher the Agent role and the embedded CP the propositional Theme role. Next, gelesen assigns the student the Agent role and the report the Theme role. Finite matrix I and embedded hat then license nominative on their respective subjects, while gelesen licenses accusative on its object. The object's determiner, adjective, and noun exhibit masculine singular accusative concord, with weak adjectival inflection after den. Finally, hat selects the participial predicate to establish the present perfect embedded construction. Matrix I carries past and third-person singular features compatible with the whole-word form sagte. No morphological decomposition of the supplied words is assumed, and punctuation is not introduced as an additional token.",
        "statement": "Establish the head-final base structure and the argument, case, and perfect-construction dependencies.",
